vmm: rebuild the vlapic trigger-mode registers (VMCS EOI-exit bitmaps) after a snapshot restore

Against FreeBSD stable/15 fabb224f2f15 plus 0001 and 0002 (independent files: sys/amd64/vmm only;
applies with patch -p1). This is the kernel module (vmm.ko), not bhyve.

With virtual interrupt delivery (APICv; hw.vmm.vmx.cap.virtual_interrupt_delivery=1) a guest EOI
for a vector exits to vmm only if that vector's bit is set in the VMCS EOI-exit bitmap; vmx_set_tmr()
keeps the bitmap equal to the vlapic TMR. The TMR is derived, never saved: vioapic_update_tmr()
recomputes it from the redirection table, and only a guest write to a redirection entry calls it
(vioapic_write() -> vm_smp_rendezvous()). A restored VM is a new VM (vmx_vcpu_init() writes
EOI_EXIT0-3 = 0); vioapic_snapshot() restores rtbl[].reg/acnt and vlapic_snapshot() the APIC page
(TMR bits included), but nothing reprograms the bitmap. So the restored guest's EOI for a
level-triggered vector is completed in hardware, vioapic_process_eoi() never runs, Remote IRR stays
set and the pin delivers nothing after its first interrupt. Seen with the ACPI SCI (IRQ 9, level):
the first GPE after a restore (patch 0002's VM-generation notify) is delivered, every later one --
the power button (SIGTERM), the next restore's notify -- is not; a snapshot taken from that guest
carries RIRR=1, so all its descendants restore already wedged. Any level-triggered INTx device is
affected the same way; virtio and ahci use MSI-X, so nothing else showed it here.

Fix: vioapic_restore_tmr() runs vioapic_update_tmr() for every vCPU, and vm_snapshot_req() calls it
after restoring STRUCT_VIOAPIC and after STRUCT_VLAPIC (bhyve restores vioapic, then vlapic; the
second rebuild sees the restored APIC ID/LDR/DFR, and the result does not depend on the order).
VM_SNAPSHOT_REQ is a VMMDEV_IOCTL_LOCK_ALL_VCPUS ioctl, one per kernel struct, so every vCPU is
frozen (vmx_set_tmr() asserts it is not running) and bhyve has activated all vCPUs before it
restores (vlapic_calcdest() only matches active vCPUs). Locking is that of the rendezvous path
(vioapic spin lock, then VMPTRLD/VMCLEAR per vector). AMD SVM installs no set_tmr op (EOIs always
trap to vlapic_process_eoi(), which reads the restored TMR from the APIC page), and neither does
VMX without virtual interrupt delivery: there the change only rewrites the TMR in the APIC page
with the value it already had.

Not changed: a snapshot taken under an unfixed vmm.ko after the first level interrupt carries
Remote IRR=1 for that pin with no EOI to come (the guest already sent it). Restore keeps that state,
as a real IO-APIC clears Remote IRR only on an EOI; telling "EOI lost" from "guest still in its
handler" would need the ISR/IRR of every vlapic and the posted-interrupt descriptors (not
snapshotted), and a wrong guess injects a duplicate interrupt. Such snapshots stay wedged on that
pin and must be retaken under the fixed module.

--- a/sys/amd64/vmm/io/vioapic.c
+++ b/sys/amd64/vmm/io/vioapic.c
@@ -543,4 +543,33 @@
 done:
 	return (ret);
 }
+
+/*
+ * Rebuild every vCPU's trigger-mode register from the restored redirection
+ * table.  At run time this happens only when the guest writes a redirection
+ * entry (vioapic_write() -> vioapic_update_tmr()), and a restored VM is a new
+ * VM: its vlapics and, with virtual interrupt delivery, its VMCS EOI-exit
+ * bitmaps start out all edge-triggered.  The APIC page restored by
+ * vlapic_snapshot() carries the TMR bits, but the EOI-exit bitmap is not part
+ * of any snapshot, so the guest's EOI for a level-triggered vector would be
+ * completed in hardware and never reach vioapic_process_eoi(): Remote IRR
+ * stays set and the pin (e.g. the ACPI SCI) delivers nothing after its first
+ * interrupt.  Called with all vCPUs frozen, after both the vioapic and the
+ * vlapic state have been restored (vlapic_set_tmr_level() matches the
+ * destination against the restored APIC ID, LDR and DFR).
+ */
+void
+vioapic_restore_tmr(struct vm *vm)
+{
+	struct vcpu *vcpu;
+	uint16_t i, maxcpus;
+
+	maxcpus = vm_get_maxcpus(vm);
+	for (i = 0; i < maxcpus; i++) {
+		vcpu = vm_vcpu(vm, i);
+		if (vcpu == NULL)
+			continue;
+		vioapic_update_tmr(vcpu, NULL);
+	}
+}
 #endif
--- a/sys/amd64/vmm/io/vioapic.h
+++ b/sys/amd64/vmm/io/vioapic.h
@@ -53,6 +53,7 @@
 #ifdef BHYVE_SNAPSHOT
 int	vioapic_snapshot(struct vioapic *vioapic,
 			 struct vm_snapshot_meta *meta);
+void	vioapic_restore_tmr(struct vm *vm);
 #endif
 
 #endif /* _KERNEL */
--- a/sys/amd64/vmm/vmm.c
+++ b/sys/amd64/vmm/vmm.c
@@ -2072,6 +2072,20 @@
 		       __func__, meta->dev_req);
 		ret = (EINVAL);
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The vlapic trigger-mode registers (and the VMCS EOI-exit bitmaps
+	 * behind them) are derived from the vioapic redirection table and the
+	 * vlapic addressing state, not saved: rebuild them once either part is
+	 * restored.  bhyve restores the vioapic before the vlapic, so the
+	 * rebuild after the vlapic is the one that counts; doing it after
+	 * both keeps the result independent of that order.  The ioctl holds
+	 * every vCPU frozen (VMMDEV_IOCTL_LOCK_ALL_VCPUS).
+	 */
+	if (ret == 0 && meta->op == VM_SNAPSHOT_RESTORE &&
+	    (meta->dev_req == STRUCT_VIOAPIC || meta->dev_req == STRUCT_VLAPIC))
+		vioapic_restore_tmr(vm);
+
 	return (ret);
 }
 
